<p>1) You can convince soldiers that you're one of them in a matter of seconds, especially given the situation (WWII soldiers would have welcomed Resistance help, which was a Dutch thing from 1941). However, if this is a &quot;normal&quot; thing, then it's likely that almost no one would consider it more than a show. However, if you want to interact and have a meaningful conversation, you need more time.</p>
<p>There are the questions of why someone would try to interact with the show and how someone who would want to be close to the guns when they appear would manage that.</p>
<p>2) There needs to be an actual story; there has to be a reason for this particular unit defending against those particular airplanes, and why people are allowed to interact with them. Was the unit losing people, and so sees allowing people to join as a necessity? Could it have been a volunteer battery? You just need some sort of story.</p>
<p>3) So what happens when you interact with the unit besides disappearing with it? And if it's happened before, what does The Foundation know? These are going to help sell your idea, so make sure you include them.</p>

							</div>

								
					
